To execute a file ./filename.sh or sh filename.sh

The main purpose of linux is security.
Despite the fact that you created the file linux has to know who is allowed to execute a file,

Chmod - grants permissions 
ch stands for change
Chmod is divided into 3 categories:
1- which user has access
2- which group has access
3- what are your permissions

To grant access to everybody we use; chmod 777 filename.sh
7 for me, 7 for my group, 7 for everyone 

But why 777, Linux used a formala called 4 2 1 
4 - read
2 - write 

1 - execute

so the 7 means read, write and execute

Comments in bash scripting is done by starting with a #

Purpose of Shell Scripting in Devops?
A devops engineer performs the following tasks;
-Infrastructure maintenance
-Code maintenance (git and linux)
-Configuration management

Shell scripting is important in DevOps because it automates system administration tasks, manages servers, runs CI/CD pipeline commands, and helps 
deploy and monitor infrastructure efficiently.

There are 7 core concepts in Scripts
1- Variables - they store values you want to reuse.
EG; 
  name="Zarah"
  echo "Hello $name"
Why DevOps uses it:
store paths
store environment variables
store container names

2️⃣ If Statements (Conditionals)
Used to make decisions in scripts.
eg; if [ -f file.txt ]
then
  echo "File exists"

DevOps use cases:
check if a service is running
check if a file exists
check deployment success

3️⃣ Loops
Loops repeat commands.
eg; for container in app db redis
do
  docker restart $container
done

4️⃣ Command Substitution
Stores command output inside variables.
eg; disk=$(df -h / | tail -1)
echo $disk
Used for:
monitoring
logs
automation scripts

5️⃣ Functions
Functions group reusable commands.
Example:
backup() {
  echo "Starting backup..."
  tar -czf backup.tar.gz /data
}
backup
Useful in deployment scripts.

6️⃣ Script Arguments
Scripts can accept inputs.
Example:
./deploy.sh production
Script:
echo "Deploying to $1"
DevOps pipelines pass arguments like this.
Used heavily in CI/CD systems like:
Jenkins
GitHub Actions

7️⃣ Exit Codes
Programs return status codes.
0 → success
1+ → error
Example:
if [ $? -eq 0 ]
then
  echo "Command succeeded"
fi
DevOps pipelines rely on this to determine success/failure.

🎯 Goal of the project

You built a simple automation script that:
1- Pulls latest code from Git
2- Stops old application
3- Builds new Docker image
4- Runs new container

This simulates a basic CI/CD deployment flow

🟢 STEP 1 — Create project folder
Command:
mkdir devops-demo1
cd devops-demo1
Why you did it: You created a dedicated workspace for your project.

Mental model: “Start a new project directory”

🟢 STEP 2 — Initialize Git repository
Command:
git init
Why: This turns your folder into a Git-tracked project using Git.

Mental model: “Start tracking changes in this project”

🟢 STEP 3 — Create project files
Command:
touch Dockerfile
touch deploy.sh

or using vim:

vim deploy.sh
Why:

You created:
Dockerfile → defines how to build the app
deploy.sh → automation script

Mental model: “Define app structure + automation logic”

🟢 STEP 4 — Write Dockerfile
Example content:
FROM node:18
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
CMD ["node", "app.js"]
Why: This tells Docker how to build your application image.

Mental model:“Recipe for building my app environment”

🟢 STEP 5 — Create deployment script
Command:
vim deploy.sh
Then add:
#!/bin/bash

echo "Pulling latest code..."
git pull

echo "Stopping old container..."
docker stop devops-app
docker rm devops-app

echo "Building new image..."
docker build -t devops-app .

echo "Running new container..."
docker run -d --name devops-app devops-app

echo "Deployment complete!"
Why this script exists

It automates deployment so you don’t manually:

pull code
rebuild app
restart container
Mental model:

“Turn manual deployment into a repeatable process”

🟢 STEP 6 — Make script executable
Command:
chmod +x deploy.sh
Why:

This allows Linux to run the file as a program.

Without this:

./deploy.sh ❌ not allowed

With this:

./deploy.sh ✅ works

🟢 STEP 7 — First Git workflow
Add files:
git add .
Why: Stages files for commit.

Commit: git commit -m "Initial project setup"
Why: Saves snapshot of project.

Connect to GitHub:
git remote add origin <repo-url>
Why: Links local repo to GitHub.

Push code: git push -u origin main
Why: Uploads your project to GitHub.

🟢 STEP 8 — Run deployment script
Command:
./deploy.sh
What happens internally:
1. Pull code
git pull

Gets latest changes from GitHub.

2. Stop container
docker stop devops-app

Stops running app.

3. Remove container
docker rm devops-app

Cleans old version.

4. Build image
docker build -t devops-app .

Creates new app version.

5. Run container
docker run -d --name devops-app devops-app

Starts new app in background.

🧠 FINAL FLOW (IMPORTANT)
mkdir project
git init
create files
write Dockerfile
write deploy script
chmod +x script
git add/commit/push
run ./deploy.sh
